research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"thm-lhopital-zero-over-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~kouba/CalcOneDIRECTORY/lhopitaldirectory/LHopital.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the 0/0 rule under the same derivative-quotient and nonvanishing-denominator-derivative hypotheses. The item uniformly packages one-sided, two-sided, infinite-endpoint, and extended-real limits and derives infinity by t=1/x, so it is a convention-rich semantic restatement rather than an exact copy.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-10-{"id":"thm-lhopital-infinity-over-infinity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~kouba/CalcOneDIRECTORY/lhopitaldirectory/LHopital.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the infinity-over-infinity rule for finite and infinite endpoints. The item consolidates endpoint modes, extended-real derivative limits, absolute divergence, and eventual fixed signs in the library's conventions. Its Cauchy-mean-value proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-taylor-polynomial-and-remainder","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html"],"rationale":"The source defines the degree-n Taylor polynomial and pointwise remainder with the same coefficients. The item adapts it to the library's canonical-natural and finite-sum notation and explicitly permits any point where the required derivatives exist.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"lem-taylor-polynomial-derivatives","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html"],"rationale":"The source states that the Taylor polynomial matches every derivative of f through order n at the centre. This is the same claim and range of orders; the termwise finite-sum calculation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"thm-taylor-schlomilch-roche-remainder","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SchloemilchRemainder.html"],"rationale":"The source gives the Schlomilch/Roche remainder formula with the same parameter and interpolating point. The item restricts the parameter to an integer between 1 and n+1, states relative-interval regularity explicitly, and uses the library's factorial and integer-power conventions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-14-{"id":"cor-taylor-lagrange-and-cauchy-remainders","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SchloemilchRemainder.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the Lagrange and Cauchy forms as the corresponding special cases of Taylor's remainder. The item packages both in one corollary under its parent theorem's relative-interval conventions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:15:{"id":"thm-taylor-peano-remainder","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.drexel.edu/~tolya/Lagrange%20remainder.pdf"],"rationale":"Grinshpan's university Real Analysis note states the Peano form under n-times differentiability at the expansion point, with remainder o((x-a)^n), matching the item's hypothesis, quantifier, and direction. The inductive quotient proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-10"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:16:{"id":"cor-taylor-remainder-bound","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.drexel.edu/~tolya/Lagrange%20remainder.pdf"],"rationale":"Grinshpan's university Real Analysis note states the Lagrange remainder formula under the corresponding derivative hypotheses; taking the displayed derivative value at the intermediate point under the uniform bound gives exactly M|x-a|^{n+1}/(n+1)!. The repository's proof is a direct local application of its preceding remainder theorem.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-10"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-17-{"id":"thm-second-derivative-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html"],"rationale":"The source gives the same pointwise second-derivative test: f'(c)=0 with positive or negative f''(c) yields a strict local minimum or maximum. The item proves it through derivative monotonicity under the same weak pointwise hypothesis.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-18-{"id":"thm-first-nonzero-derivative-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~voronov/5615-20/pdfs/12-2-1.pdf"],"rationale":"The source gives the higher-derivative test with the parity and sign alternatives. The item replaces a neighbourhood C^n hypothesis by the sharper Peano-at-the-point hypothesis and spells out the odd case's increase-through-the-point conclusion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-19-{"id":"rem-darboux-lhopital-taylor-scope","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"This is a repository-specific scope statement about the exact conventions and deliberate omissions of the current page, not an established mathematical proposition. Each mathematical cross-reference and every asserted omission was checked directly against the cited on-disk item and page.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"fs-zero-second-derivative-decides-extremum","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html","https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~voronov/5615-20/pdfs/12-2-1.pdf"],"rationale":"The literature states that a zero second derivative is inconclusive and gives the higher-derivative test that distinguishes the three outcomes. The item combines the standard witnesses x^4, -x^4, and x^3 into one explicit refutation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"ex-piecewise-polynomial-periodic-oscillator","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The normalized quartic Hermite cell 16u^2(1-u)^2, its periodic gluing, and the chosen derivative values are a bespoke local construction. Direct polynomial differentiation gives matching value and first derivative at every seam, periodicity and boundedness are immediate from the fractional-part definition, and substituting the displayed quarter-points gives derivative values 3 and -3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"ex-differentiable-function-with-discontinuous-derivative","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The witness x^2 psi(1/x) uses the preceding bespoke polynomial oscillator rather than the classical trigonometric example. The difference quotient at zero is bounded by |x| times a fixed bound for psi, while the product/chain calculation and the oscillator's recurring derivative values directly show discontinuity of F' at zero.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
--
