research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-geometric-function-taylor-remainder.result.json:16:  "tail": "```text\nVERDICT: CLEAN\n\nBOUNDARY: The public claim holds for all real x ≠ 1; the Taylor polynomial identity extends to x = 1 as well. The exact remainder matches the algebraic identity 1/(1−x) − Σ_{j=0}^n x^j = x^{n+1}/(1−x), requiring no convergence conditions. Standard boundary cases (empty, zero, one, degenerate, endpoints) are inapplicable or satisfied, and the proof does not rely on any hidden choice.\n\nSOURCES: All 10 cited items were inspected: def-taylor-polynomial-and-remainder, cor-taylor-remainder-bound, lem-finite-sum-laws, thm-algebra-of-derivatives, thm-chain-rule, lem-derivative-of-a-power, def-factorial-and-falling-factorial, def-canonical-natural, lem-of-naturals-positive, thm-induction-principle. Each provides exactly the statement required for the target’s steps (derivative rules, factorial embedding, telescoping sums, induction).\n\nCHECKED: Title, public statement, Facts & Assumptions, every numbered step (1.1, 2.1, 3.1), and the associated derivations were examined; no fatal defect was found.\n```"
